WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.540
Late Wednesday, after initially trailing,

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.880
a voter initiative appears to have been approved

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.380
to make Denver the first US city to decriminalize magic mushrooms.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.700
And it's thanks to these people at this election night party.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.600
We all know that this goes way beyond Denver,
way beyond Denver.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.200
Right now this thing is a coast to coast movement.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.140
Kevin Matthews is the campaign manager behind
Decriminalize Denver

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.120
the political activist group that got the
measure on the ballot.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.900
He also personally testifies to the power
of psilocybin mushrooms

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.780
which he says helped cure his depression.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.940
I was a cadet at the US Military Academy

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:45.940
and my life dream was to serve my country
as career army officer

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.000
and while I was at the academy I was diagnosed
with major depression

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:55.240
and then I eventually received a medical retirement
from the army because of depression

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.740
and it felt like that my purpose had basically
crumbled apart and disappeared.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:08.800
Then I met an amazing group of friends here
in Denver who responsibly introduced me to psilocybin.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.580
And it was this one experience that I had,
just cleared the fog,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.860
and so after that experience, the benefits
lasted for weeks,

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.740
that really enabled me to step outside the
box of depression

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.880
and start to create a new life for myself

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.420
without needing to go back to using the mushrooms again.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.840
Campaign political coordinater Cindy Sovine

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:34.200
brought her experience lobbying for increased
access to cannabis to the psilocybin mushroom fight.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.880
If there is anything that this country should
be talking about after we do this in Denver

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.300
is that we have set the container to decriminalize
all drugs.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.440
This is about harm reduction and we need to
start there

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.840
before we start talking about legalizing any
more

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:53.040
because we should not be giving people the
ability to make a profit off the sale of a legal product

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.660
while we're still putting people in jail for
using that product.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.200
Denver police have averaged an arrest of about
50 people a year for the past three years

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.640
for possessing or selling psilocybin mushrooms

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.580
and prosecuted about 11 of those cases last
year according tot he Washington Post.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.560
But Sovine says that while the numbers may
be small, the personal impact is huge.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.600
If you are the mother or the son or the brother
or the father of one of those individuals

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.820
it is still a priority, in fact one of the
folks very much involved in our campaign

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.400
is one of those people who was arrested and
thrown in jail

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.120
and still to this day has ramifications within
his life

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.380
for what he can and cannot do because of psilocybin.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.200
Sovine also says she believed Denver already
had the psychedelic culture

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.020
necessary to be the first to try decriminalization.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.480
A culture which is on display in the city's
rhino art district

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.440
that features buildings and an entire alley
covered with psychedelic street art.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.500
The psychedelics are already here, there's
already a psychedelic renaissance happening

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.100
we're seeing different types of providers
that are coming in

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.920
and now using this, Innate Pathways, MAPS up
in Boulder who are doing all these types of research.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.140
There's a lot more information available here
in this community and locally around what's happening

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.820
and they're being used, they're being used
in the music scene they're being used all over.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:18.320
We are looking at that and recognizing that
we don't think that people should be going to jail

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.100
or otherwise losing their livelihoods for
the result of using this substance.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.740
Colorado was one of the first two US states
to legalize recreational cannabis sales

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.520
which it did by voter initiative in 2012,

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.920
putting it on the frontier of American drug policy then and now.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.680
This is the best place to do this in the country
right now.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:43.680
Denver and maybe the state of Colorado is
in many ways, at least with drug policy,

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.820
embracing more of this libertarian concept
that as long as I'm not causing harm to self or others

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.300
we should be, we should have the freedom to
use whatever substances we choose

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.720
and put into our bodies whatever we choose.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.600
Denver's decriminalization initiative makes the possession or use of psilocybin

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.380
the city's lowest law-enforcement priority
and prohibit the city from spending resources

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.260
to impose criminal penalties for the personal use

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.420
and personal possession of psilocybin mushrooms

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.000
by persons 21 years of age and older.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.860
and also establishes a panel to review the effects of the change in law.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.380
It does not legalize commercial sales of mushrooms

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.180
leaving a potentially large gray market.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.040
Sovine says her experience in cannabis
legalization,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.760
which progressed from medical to recreational
in every state that's legalized it,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.800
convinced her that decriminalization rather
than medicalization

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.960
is actually the far more radical and liberatory
path to take.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.360
Maybe we want to see doctors being able to
prescribe psilocybin

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.140
but is that the only way we want to be able
to access it?

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.320
Should we still be going to jail and having
it be a criminal offense

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.000
outside of having a physician prescribe it
for you lawfully?

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.960
I think that the core tenet of the reason
why the campaign became what it was today

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.260
was because we don't necessarily think that
going through the process

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.800
necessarily means access to all patients.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.380
Groups like the Multidisciplinary Association
For Psychedelic Studies or MAPS

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.920
have spent nearly 33 years funding the study
of psychedelics

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.160
for treating mental and emotional problems.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.520
And that work is only recently reaching fruition
with the study of the psychedelic MDMA

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.740
to treat post traumatic stress disorder about
the enter the final phase of FDA clinical trials

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:29.340
and the organization is hopeful it will achieve
full approval for use in clinical settings by 2021.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:35.400
Scientists like Charles Grob at Johns Hopkins
have also studied psilocybin in a clinical setting

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.480
and found a measurable decrease in death anxiety
in terminal cancer patients

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.080
dosed with the drug sometimes just a single
time.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:48.480
Subjects reported being more engaged in
their lives,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.320
getting a renewed sense of self and a renewed
sense of meaning and purpose

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.000
to the time they had remaining.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
But advocates say the more radical decriminalization
movement

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.200
complements rather than undermines the work
of groups like MAPS.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.260
Psilocybin, mushrooms, have the potential
to address

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.700
the five psychiatric symptoms that we're facing
as a country.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:12.400
and those things are depression, anxiety,
addiction, PTSD and pain.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.760
The research is amazing, it costs a lot of
money and takes a lot of time

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.960
and the fact that people are being criminalized
today as we speak

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.460
is a big motivation for why we're doing this
work.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:30.040
On the horizon activists are working to put
statewide psilocybin decriminalization

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.780
on the ballots in Oregon and California in 2020.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.440
Both Sovine and Matthews believe that what happened in Denver won't stay in Denver.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.500
Part of the team from California is here tonight, they came out to support us.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.260
And I know the petitioners in Oregon.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:53.800
The best advice I can offer is find volunteers who are committed to this,

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.540
fundraising is key,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.300
really respect and honor the research and the researchers

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:02.780
who have dedicated their entire lives to this work, they need to be a part of the conversation.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.280
It starts by forcing the conversation

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:12.280
and protecting access, whether you're using this plant for a medical reason

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.320
or a spiritual reason or a recreational reason

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.180
you should not be going to jail or losing your children for it.

